 4. 🎯 Ossoff tests Dem appetite for going low 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Photo: Ben Hendren/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The firestorm over Sen. Jon Ossoff's suggestive remark about President Trump's right-hand woman has raised a bigger question about the type of campaign Democrats will run in 2028, Axios' Mike Zapler writes. 

 Why it matters: Trump has made schoolyard taunts and personal insults a staple of presidential politics. One of Democrats' big strategic calls in 2028 is whether to fight on Trump's terms — or bet that voters are exhausted and ready for something a bit more dignified. At a campaign rally Sunday, Ossoff went a step beyond the cutting one-liners he's delighted Democratic audiences with, veering into personal insinuation.

 "He doesn't want to do the job," Ossoff said of Trump . "He wants to build his ballroom and travel with Natalie on their apparently defenseless flying palace gifted by the emir of Qatar." The Natalie he referenced is Trump's 35-year-old aide Natalie Harp, who is constantly by his side and serves as a gatekeeper for what he reads. It was widely interpreted as insinuating that their relationship goes beyond work. Trump and his aides unloaded: The president compared Ossoff to Pee-wee Herman. White House communications director Steven Cheung called him "the biggest cuck loser in politics." Other Republicans called him sexist. 🎬 The big picture: Ossoff's remark generated enormous attention and highlighted an emerging divide among top Democrats that's more about temperament than ideology.

 California Gov. Gavin Newsom — a potential 2028 contender — and Ossoff, who insists he isn't, are staking out the pugilist end of the spectrum. The other side: Pennsylvania Gov. Josh Shapiro and Kentucky Gov. Andy Beshear, both possible 2028 presidential candidates, have cut more restrained profiles — a "let's just focus on the job" ethos, captured by Shapiro's "get sh*t done" mantra, minus the drama.

